      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.stselectronicrecyclinginc.com/bakersfield-ca#faqpage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What areas do you serve?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS provides free pickup across Bakersfield and the greater Kern County area for qualifying business and institutional volumes. Our secure fleet also serves commercial employers throughout the San Joaquin Valley on scheduled weekday collection routes. Organizations searching for electronics recycling near me throughout Bakersfield find STS provides free pickup in Rosedale, Oildale, Downtown Bakersfield, and Southwest Bakersfield, plus neighboring Kern County communities including Delano, Wasco, Shafter, Taft, Tehachapi, Arvin, and Ridgecrest. Offices, plants, and clinics typically receive same-week windows after volume is confirmed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What certifications do you hold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS follows NIST SP 800-88 media sanitization and DoD 5220.22-M destruction practices for retired hard drives and servers collected from Bakersfield organizations. We also support HIPAA 45 CFR §164.312 requirements for healthcare equipment collected from Bakersfield hospitals and clinics. Business Associate Agreements are available when a healthcare client requires one. Chain of custody documentation and certificates of destruction typically list asset serials so Kern County compliance teams can support internal reviews and outside audits."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is pickup really free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Business pickup is free for qualifying volumes of retired electronics collected from Bakersfield offices, plants, and warehouses. After a short inventory review, our logistics team confirms eligibility and typically schedules same-week collection when capacity allows. Complimentary pickup covers Bakersfield and the greater Kern County area. No hidden fees apply to those qualifying collections. Share a rough count of PCs, monitors, and copiers so we can confirm whether the lot qualifies, along with dock hours, forklift needs, and floor access."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do you provide audit documentation?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Each Bakersfield engagement includes full chain of custody records and a certificate of destruction for sanitized media. Accurate reporting typically lists asset serials so your compliance team can support internal reviews and outside audits. Certificates of destruction are typically issued within 48 hours after media sanitization is complete. Serial lists are included when tags are readable. A manifest is created as equipment is loaded, and counts and visible serials are recorded before the truck leaves that Kern County site."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does pickup take?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most Bakersfield organizations typically receive same-week pickup after we confirm volume, dock access, and site contacts. On-site collection time depends on asset count, building layout, and whether witnessed destruction is requested by your team. A written scope is sent before the crew is assigned. Multi-building employers in Kern County can place more than one stop on a single run. Plant and clinic sites use the same documented process, and palletized loads reduce dock time when equipment is staged in a single room."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens to my equipment after pickup?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Equipment collected in Bakersfield is securely staged, serialized, and then transported to our 200,000 sq ft processing operation. Data-bearing devices are sanitized or physically destroyed, and remaining commodities then enter documented downstream recovery with complete reporting. Methods follow NIST 800-88 purge and destroy guidance. You receive chain of custody records and a certificate of destruction, typically within 48 hours of processing. Sustainable processing keeps retired electronics out of landfills and supports ESG reporting for Kern County organizations."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What electronics can be recycled in Bakersfield?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Bakersfield organizations can recycle computers, laptops, phones, servers, printers, copiers, monitors, networking gear, and legacy electronics through STS. Data-bearing devices are sanitized before commodity recovery, and qualifying commercial volumes typically include free pickup across Kern County. Ink, toner, and old electronics ride the same scheduled route. Desktop towers, rugged field laptops, rack servers, cell phones, tablets, floor copiers, and LCD or LED displays are accepted. Screens are processed as universal waste under California DTSC and CalRecycle rules."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is data safe during Bakersfield electronics recycling?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "According to NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 2 guidelines, media sanitization requires verification of purge-level overwrite or physical destruction, included in every STS engagement. Under HIPAA 45 CFR §164.312 requirements, electronic PHI on disposed devices must be rendered irretrievable, and STS destruction procedures support this standard. Business Associate Agreements are available when a healthcare client requires one. On-site hard drive shredding is available when witnessed destruction is required, with serial-level records for each drive collected in Bakersfield."
          }
        }
      ]
